course: biology
lesson: 236
title: Animal minds: elephants' memory, feelings and the mouse myth
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: «An elephant never forgets» is an old saying, and like many old sayings it is partly true: elephants have excellent memories, though not perfect ones.
explain: An elephant family is led by its oldest female, the matriarch, who remembers water holes, paths and dangers over decades.
explain: Elephants also seem to have strong feelings: they comfort a frightened calf and stop at the bones of a dead elephant, touching them gently.
explain: Think of the cartoon where a huge elephant jumps on a chair, screaming at a tiny mouse: it is funny, but there is no good evidence that real elephants fear mice.
fact: Elephants often touch the bones of dead elephants gently with trunks and feet and pay much less attention to bones of other animals; scientists think they may feel something like grief.
fact: In a drought in Tanzania, herds with older matriarchs who remembered an earlier drought survived better.
fact: There is no good evidence that elephants fear mice; like us, they may be startled by any small thing moving suddenly near their feet.
fact: Elephants in Kenya can tell the voices of Maasai men, who sometimes hunt them, from the voices of women and boys, and they fear only the men's voices.
fact: Elephants can hold a paintbrush in their trunk, but painting elephants are trained and often guided by keepers, and many experts think the shows are for tourists.
fact: An elephant's trunk has tens of thousands of muscle bundles and can hold things as delicately as fingers.
fact: Elephants use branches to swat flies and scratch, and a zoo elephant pushed a box under a branch and stood on it to reach fruit.
fact: Elephants comfort a scared elephant by touching it with their trunks and making soft chirping sounds.
fact: An Asian elephant called Happy touched a mark on her head that she could only see in a big mirror, but some elephants do not pass the test.
fact: Elephant families walk behind the matriarch along old paths that lead to water, salt licks and fruit trees.
fact: African elephants run from the buzz of angry bees, which sting the trunk and around the eyes, so Kenyan farmers hang beehives on fences to protect crops.
fact: An elephant brain weighs about 5 kilograms, the largest of any land animal, but a big body needs a big brain, so size alone does not show cleverness.
exercise: What do elephants do at the bones of a dead elephant? | Touch them gently and may stay a long time | Kick them away | Eat them | Ignore them completely and walk straight past without stopping
exercise: Is «an elephant never forgets» true? | Partly: elephants have excellent but not perfect memories | Fully: they remember every single second of their long lives | Not at all: they forget everything | Only baby elephants remember
exercise: Who leads an elephant family? | The oldest female, the matriarch | The biggest and the strongest male | The youngest calf | A different elephant each day
exercise: Why did herds with older matriarchs survive a drought better? | The old females remembered an earlier drought and where to find water | Old elephants drink less | Young elephants had stayed behind | They were bigger and stronger, so they could walk for longer without water
exercise: Are elephants afraid of mice? | There is no good evidence that they are | Yes, every elephant in the world fears mice | Only African elephants fear mice, not Asian ones | Yes, because mice climb right up their trunks
exercise: Why might an elephant jump when a mouse runs past? | Any sudden small movement near its feet can startle it | Mice bite elephants' toes | It fears the squeak | Elephants hate all grey animals, because they look like rivals
exercise: Whose voices did elephants in Kenya fear? | Maasai men's, as men sometimes hunt them | Women's | Small boys' | All people's voices, equally, whoever was speaking
exercise: What is true about elephants painting pictures? | They are trained and often guided by keepers | They paint landscapes of their homes | They paint only when they are happy in the wild | They teach each other to paint
exercise: Why can an elephant hold a brush so delicately? | Its trunk has tens of thousands of muscle bundles | Its trunk has bones inside, just like fingers | Its trunk is glued to the brush | Its tusks hold the brush
exercise: What does an elephant use a branch for? | Swatting flies and scratching itself | Digging tunnels | Building nests in the trees | Fighting lions only
exercise: How do elephants comfort a scared calf? | Touch it with their trunks and make soft chirping sounds | Push it away | Leave it alone | Trumpet loudly at it until it stops being scared and runs off
exercise: What did the elephant Happy do in the mirror test? | Touched a mark on her head she could see only in the mirror | Broke the mirror | Kissed the mirror | Painted a picture of herself on the mirror with the tip of her trunk
exercise: Where do old elephant paths lead? | To water, salt licks and fruit trees | To towns | Nowhere special | To the sea
exercise: Why do Kenyan farmers hang beehives on fences? | Elephants avoid angry bees, so crops are protected | To feed the elephants honey | To catch elephants | To keep the bees warm in the cold rainy season
exercise: Where do bees sting elephants? | On the soft parts of the trunk and round the eyes | On the thick, tough skin all along the elephant's back | On the tusks | On the toenails
exercise: How heavy is an elephant's brain? | About 5 kilograms | About 50 grams, as light as an egg | About 50 kilograms | About the same as a human brain
exercise: Does a big brain alone prove an animal is clever? | No, a big body needs a big brain to control it | Yes, always, the biggest brain is always the cleverest | Yes, but only for elephants, because their brains are special | No, because brains do not do any of the thinking at all
test: A cartoon elephant leaps onto a chair, screaming at a mouse. What would a real elephant probably do? | Maybe step around it or be briefly startled, as by anything sudden | Faint at once | Run for miles across the plain, trumpeting in terror and never come back | Climb a tree
test: «elephant painted a flower at the zoo, so it's an artist innit» What is more accurate? | It was trained and guided, and the show is mostly for tourists | Yes, elephants paint in the wild | Elephants paint better than people | Elephants paint flowers to send messages to other elephants far away
test: What would happen if the matriarch of a herd were killed by poachers before a drought? | The herd might not know where the old water holes are | The herd would get faster | Nothing, because every elephant in the herd already knows the way | The calves would lead better
test: Farmers play a recording of buzzing bees near a field. What will the elephants probably do? | Walk away from the sound | Come closer to eat the honey | Fall asleep to the humming | Start trumpeting a song back
test: «a whale's brain is massive, so a whale must be cleverer than me» What is wrong? | A big body needs a big brain, so size alone does not prove cleverness | Nothing, bigger brains always win | Whales have no brains | Only small brains are clever
test: A woman and a man from a group that sometimes hunts elephants talk near a herd in Kenya. Whose voice makes them nervous? | The man's | The woman's | Both equally | Neither of them
test: «elephants never forget, so my nan's elephant from the circus still remembers her» What is the best answer? | Elephants have very good but not perfect memories, so maybe | Yes, elephants remember everything forever | No, elephants forget in a day | Only if she fed it peanuts every single day when it was in the circus
test: Count them: a mother elephant and her 2 calves walk by. How many legs and how many trunks? | 12 legs and 3 trunks | 12 legs and 1 trunk | 8 legs and 3 trunks | 16 legs and 3 trunks
test: Count them: 4 elephants and 3 people stand by a water hole. How many ears? | 14 ears | 8 ears | 16 ears | 7 ears
test: A zoo elephant cannot reach fruit on a high branch. A box lies nearby. What did one zoo elephant do? | Pushed the box under the branch and stood on it | Waited for the fruit to fall | Threw the box at the tree until all the fruit came down | Gave up and slept
test: A herd passes the bones of a buffalo and then the bones of an elephant. Where will it probably stop longest? | At the elephant bones | At the buffalo bones first | At neither, it walks past both | It will run away from both
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the alleged fear of mice exhibited by elephants? | No sound evidence supports it, an elephant being startled by any sudden movement near its feet | It is universally observed in all elephants | It arises from the habit of mice of entering the trunk | It is confined to elephants kept in circuses
exam: Identify the principal reason for which elephant herds led by elder matriarchs exhibited superior survival during a drought in Tanzania. | The matriarchs retained a memory of an earlier drought and of sources of water | The elder matriarchs required less water | The herds were of greater size | The herds were provisioned with water and food by human beings throughout the drought
exam: Ascertain the purpose for which beehives are suspended upon fences by farmers in Kenya. | The deterrence of elephants from the consumption of crops | The provision of honey to elephants in exchange for their departure | The capture of elephants | The warming of the bees
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the behaviour of elephants towards the remains of deceased elephants. | They touch the bones gently and attend to them more than to the bones of other species | They consume the bones | They avoid all bones without distinction | They bury every bone they encounter, whether of their own kind or of any other species whatsoever
